<PageShell title="Reflection on FPAS">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      When I first arrived at Doane University, I recognized quickly that most of the online biology courses
      were not up to the necessary level of quality and academic rigor. There was very little alignment of
      the online offerings or overlap with on-ground residential courses and there was a large disconnect
      between on-ground and online faculty. My role was absolutely necessary to fix this shortcoming,
      improve the online course offerings, and serve as a conduit between the residential and online
      faculty. After three years in my role, I feel like I have accomplished my goal of leading the charge of
      course redevelopment, faculty and student mentoring, and to harmonize the relationship between
      online and on-ground faculty.
    </p>
    <p>
      My first three years at Doane have given me a strong foundation in online teaching and have helped me
      develop courses that are organized, accessible, and student-centered. As I move into the next stage of
      my professional growth, I want to be more intentional in advancing my courses beyond clear structure
      and consistent delivery. My goal is to design learning experiences that promote deeper thinking,
      require authentic application of course concepts, provide more meaningful individualized feedback,
      and help students engage responsibly with emerging technologies, particularly artificial intelligence.
      This next phase of development will focus on strengthening both the intellectual rigor of my courses
      and the quality of student engagement within the online learning environment.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">1. Strengthen individualized and formative feedback.</h2>
    <p>
      I plan to continue improving the quality and purpose of my written feedback. Rather than focusing
      primarily on identifying what is missing or incorrect, I want my feedback to function more as a
      teaching tool. My goal is to provide comments and questions that prompt students to revisit their
      reasoning, clarify misunderstandings, and make stronger connections to course concepts. By making
      feedback more individualized, formative, and reflective, I hope to help students move beyond task
      completion and toward deeper conceptual understanding.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">
      2. Replace less effective assignments with authentic, application-based learning.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I will continue to evaluate assignments for alignment with learning outcomes, academic rigor, and
      their ability to promote meaningful engagement. As part of this process, I will identify assignments
      that may be vulnerable to superficial completion or that do not fully support the level of thinking I
      want students to demonstrate. For example, the current mitosis assignment is one I intend to replace
      with a case-based activity that requires students to apply biological concepts to a realistic
      scenario. This shift will allow students to demonstrate not only that they understand course content,
      but that they can use that knowledge to analyze problems, interpret evidence, and explain biological
      processes in context.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">
      3. Expand instructor-created instructional videos with greater intentionality.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I plan to continue developing instructor-created videos as a way to support student learning and
      instructor presence in the online classroom. Moving forward, I will create longer instructional videos
      selectively for topics that require more detailed explanation, modeling, or step-by-step conceptual
      development. At the same time, I will retain shorter videos when they are better suited for focused
      clarification, assignment guidance, or quick reinforcement of key concepts. My goal is not simply to
      increase the number or length of videos, but to use video strategically to support difficult concepts,
      reduce confusion, and strengthen students’ connection to the course.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">
      4. Develop students’ critical AI literacy and responsible use of emerging technologies.
    </h2>
    <p>
      As artificial intelligence becomes increasingly embedded in students’ academic and professional lives,
      I want to move beyond an approach focused only on preventing or limiting AI use. While academic
      integrity remains essential, I also recognize the importance of helping students develop the skills to
      evaluate AI-generated information critically. I plan to design assignments that require students to
      compare AI-generated explanations with peer-reviewed scientific sources, identify inaccuracies or
      omissions, and reflect on the strengths and limitations of AI as a learning tool. One activity I am
      considering would ask students to generate a biological response using AI, independently research the
      topic using credible scientific literature, and then evaluate the accuracy, completeness, and
      reliability of the AI response. This approach would allow students to practice disciplinary thinking
      while also developing responsible AI literacy.
    </p>
    <p>
      In addition, I plan to create rubrics that explicitly address students’ use of AI-related skills where
      appropriate. These rubrics will assess not only content knowledge, but also students’ ability to
      verify information, cite sources appropriately, explain their reasoning, and demonstrate that
      submitted work reflects authentic student learning. A broader goal in redesigning these assignments is
      to make deliberate decisions about when AI use should be enhanced, promoted, limited, or restricted
      based on the learning outcomes of the course. By doing so, I hope to reduce misuse of AI while
      preparing students to engage with these tools ethically and critically.
    </p>

    <h2 class="subsection-title">5. Increase engagement in professional development.</h2>
    <p>
      I recognize that professional development is an area in which I want to be more intentional and
      consistent. Moving forward, I plan to increase my participation in CETL programming and seek additional
      opportunities focused on online pedagogy, assessment design, meaningful feedback, instructional
      design, and AI in education. Continued professional development will help me refine my teaching
      practices, stay current with emerging challenges in higher education, and make evidence-informed
      decisions as I revise and strengthen my courses.
    </p>

    <p>
      Overall, my next stage of development will focus on building courses that are not only well organized
      and supportive, but also more intellectually challenging, reflective, and responsive to the changing
      educational landscape. I want my students to leave these courses with stronger content knowledge,
      improved critical thinking skills, greater confidence in applying biological concepts, and a more
      responsible understanding of how to engage with emerging technologies in academic and professional
      contexts.
    </p>
  </div>

